      "artist": "UCL Mathematical &amp; Physical Sciences from London, UK",
      "description": "The image shows a nano-device fabricated using metal deposition and electron beam lithography at a clean room facility at the London Centre for Nanotechnology. The device comprises of 6 metallic leads which are on or near a carbon nanotube (white, wire-like entity). A carbon nanotube consists only of carbon atoms and has typical diameter of 1 nm (0.000001 mm). Such a device is cooled to ultra-low temperatures (thousandths of a degree above absolute zero) and it is used for nanotechnology experiments that aim towards advances in the field of quantum computation.\n\nImage belongs to Pavlos Apostolidis (London Centre for Nanotechnology, Department of Physics &amp; Astronomy).",
      "dateCreated": "2018-01-16 15:05",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
